js-async-basics.md:
## Why it exists

Waiting is normal in programs: for a network reply, a file, a database, a timer, a click. If JavaScript stopped everything while it waited, a web page would freeze and a Node.js server could serve only one visitor at a time. So JavaScript runs your code on **one thread** and uses **asynchronous** operations instead: you start the slow thing, give JavaScript something to run **later** when it finishes, and carry on. The language has gone through three styles for expressing "later": **callbacks**, **promises**, and **async/await**. You will meet all three in real code, so you need to read each and know why the newer ones exist.

**Mental model:** a restaurant with one waiter. The waiter takes your order (starts the work), hands it to the kitchen (the network, the disk, a timer) and immediately serves other tables. When the kitchen rings the bell, the waiter brings the food (runs your "later" code). The waiter is never idle while food cooks, but also never does two things at the exact same instant. If the waiter stops to stare at one pot, every table waits.

{{dg:0}}

## How it works

### The order things run in

JavaScript finishes the current synchronous code first. Then it runs **microtasks** (promise callbacks), and only after that does it pick the next **timer or I/O callback** from the task queue. This small program shows the order. Run it with `node order.mjs` (all scripts here are ES modules, hence the `.mjs` extension):

```javascript
console.log("1 sync start");
setTimeout(() => console.log("4 timeout, 0 ms"), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log("3 promise callback"));
console.log("2 sync end");
```

```text
$ node order.mjs
1 sync start
2 sync end
3 promise callback
4 timeout, 0 ms
```

`setTimeout(fn, 0)` does not mean "now"; it means "as soon as the current code and the microtasks are done". The promise callback ran first even though it was written second. You do not need to memorise the queues; remember that **code after an `await` or inside `.then` never runs in the same breath as the code that started it**.

### Style 1: callbacks

A **callback** is a function you pass to be called when the work finishes. Node.js popularised the **error-first** convention: the first argument is an error (or `null`), the second is the result.

```javascript
function getUser(id, callback) {
  setTimeout(() => {
    if (id !== 1) return callback(new Error(`user ${id} not found`));
    callback(null, { id, name: "Ada" });
  }, 10);
}

function getOrders(user, callback) {
  setTimeout(() => callback(null, [{ id: 7, total: 30 }, { id: 8, total: 12 }]), 10);
}

function show(userId) {
  getUser(userId, (err, user) => {
    if (err) return console.log("failed:", err.message);
    getOrders(user, (err, orders) => {
      if (err) return console.log("failed:", err.message);
      const total = orders.reduce((sum, o) => sum + o.total, 0);
      console.log(`${user.name} has ${orders.length} orders worth ${total}`);
    });
  });
}

show(1);
show(2);
```

```text
$ node callbacks.mjs
failed: user 2 not found
Ada has 2 orders worth 42
```

It works, and the output order is an early hint of asynchrony: the failure for user 2 printed **before** the success for user 1 because it needed fewer steps. But see the shape of `show`: every dependent step nests one level deeper, and every level repeats the error check. With five steps this becomes the "pyramid of doom" (**callback hell**), and a forgotten `return` or a missing error check is easy to miss.

### Style 2: promises

A **promise** is an object that stands for a value that is not ready yet. It is **pending**, then becomes either **fulfilled** with a value or **rejected** with an error (it settles once and never changes). You attach handlers with `.then(onValue)`, `.catch(onError)` and `.finally(always)`. Handlers can return new promises, so steps **chain** flat instead of nesting, and **one `.catch` at the end handles an error from any earlier step**.

### Style 3: async/await

`async function` always returns a promise. Inside it, `await somePromise` pauses *that function* (not the whole program) until the promise settles, then gives you the value, or throws the rejection so you can use ordinary `try/catch/finally`. It is promises with a calmer syntax: the same flow reads top to bottom.

```javascript
const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, ms));

function getUser(id) {
  return sleep(10).then(() => {
    if (id !== 1) throw new Error(`user ${id} not found`);
    return { id, name: "Ada" };
  });
}

const getOrders = (user) => sleep(10).then(() => [{ id: 7, total: 30 }, { id: 8, total: 12 }]);

// 1. the same flow as a promise chain
getUser(1)
  .then(getOrders)
  .then((orders) => console.log("chain:", orders.length, "orders"))
  .catch((err) => console.log("chain failed:", err.message))
  .finally(() => console.log("chain finished"));

// 2. the same flow with async/await
async function show(userId) {
  try {
    const user = await getUser(userId);
    const orders = await getOrders(user);
    const total = orders.reduce((sum, o) => sum + o.total, 0);
    console.log(`await: ${user.name} has ${orders.length} orders worth ${total}`);
  } catch (err) {
    console.log("await failed:", err.message);
  } finally {
    console.log(`await finished for user ${userId}`);
  }
}

await show(1);
await show(2);
```

```text
$ node promises.mjs
chain: 2 orders
chain finished
await: Ada has 2 orders worth 42
await finished for user 1
await failed: user 2 not found
await finished for user 2
```

Both versions did the same work in the same order, and a failure (user 2) jumped straight to the `catch`. In modern JavaScript (ES modules, and Node.js 14.8 and later) you can also use **top-level `await`**, as the bottom of this script does, so you do not need to wrap everything in a function.

{{dg:1}}

### Doing several things at once

Two `await`s in a row run **one after another**. If the tasks do not depend on each other, start them together and wait for all of them. The promise combinators are the tools:

- `Promise.all([...])` waits for all; **rejects as soon as one rejects**.
- `Promise.allSettled([...])` waits for all and reports each outcome (never rejects).
- `Promise.race([...])` settles with the first one to settle (success or failure). It is the basis of timeouts.
- `Promise.any([...])` fulfils with the first **success** and rejects only if all fail.

```javascript
const sleep = (ms, value) => new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve(value), ms));
const fail = (ms, message) => new Promise((_, reject) => setTimeout(() => reject(new Error(message)), ms));
const rounded = (start) => `${Math.round((performance.now() - start) / 100) * 100} ms`;

let start = performance.now();
const a = await sleep(200, "a");
const b = await sleep(200, "b");
const c = await sleep(200, "c");
console.log("one after another:", [a, b, c], "took about", rounded(start));

start = performance.now();
const all = await Promise.all([sleep(200, "a"), sleep(200, "b"), sleep(200, "c")]);
console.log("Promise.all:      ", all, "took about", rounded(start));

try {
  await Promise.all([sleep(100, "ok"), fail(50, "second task broke")]);
} catch (err) {
  console.log("Promise.all rejects as soon as one fails:", err.message);
}

const settled = await Promise.allSettled([sleep(50, "ok"), fail(50, "boom")]);
console.log("allSettled:", settled.map((r) => r.status + (r.reason ? ` (${r.reason.message})` : "")));

console.log("race (first to finish):", await Promise.race([sleep(150, "slow"), sleep(50, "fast")]));
console.log("any (first success):  ", await Promise.any([fail(10, "x"), sleep(60, "second try worked")]));

async function withTimeout(promise, ms) {
  return Promise.race([promise, fail(ms, `timed out after ${ms} ms`)]);
}
try {
  await withTimeout(sleep(500, "too slow"), 100);
} catch (err) {
  console.log(err.message);
}
```

```text
$ node parallel.mjs
one after another: [ 'a', 'b', 'c' ] took about 600 ms
Promise.all:       [ 'a', 'b', 'c' ] took about 200 ms
Promise.all rejects as soon as one fails: second task broke
allSettled: [ 'fulfilled', 'rejected (boom)' ]
race (first to finish): fast
any (first success):   second try worked
timed out after 100 ms
```

Three tasks of 200 ms took about 600 ms one after another and about 200 ms with `Promise.all`: the work was **concurrent**, not parallel in the sense of multiple CPU cores (one thread still runs the JavaScript; the waiting happens outside it). Note also that `Promise.race` with a failing timer is a hand-made timeout, which is why the next example uses the built-in `AbortSignal.timeout`.

## Worked example

Goal: call an HTTP API with the built-in **`fetch`**, treat HTTP errors as errors, and give up when the server is too slow. The script starts a tiny server inside itself on a random free port, so it runs with no setup (Node.js 18 or later has `fetch` built in; this was run on Node 22).

```javascript
import http from "node:http";

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  if (req.url === "/user") {
    res.setHeader("Content-Type", "application/json");
    res.end(JSON.stringify({ id: 1, name: "Ada" }));
  } else if (req.url === "/slow") {
    setTimeout(() => res.end("finally"), 1000);
  } else {
    res.statusCode = 404;
    res.setHeader("Content-Type", "application/json");
    res.end(JSON.stringify({ error: "not found" }));
  }
});
await new Promise((resolve) => server.listen(0, "127.0.0.1", resolve));
const base = `http://127.0.0.1:${server.address().port}`;

const ok = await fetch(`${base}/user`);
console.log("status", ok.status, "| ok:", ok.ok, "| body:", await ok.json());

const missing = await fetch(`${base}/nope`); // does NOT throw on 404
console.log("status", missing.status, "| ok:", missing.ok, "| fetch did not reject");

async function getJson(url) {
  const res = await fetch(url, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(300) });
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status} for ${url.replace(base, "")}`);
  return res.json();
}
for (const path of ["/user", "/nope", "/slow"]) {
  try {
    console.log(path, "->", await getJson(base + path));
  } catch (err) {
    console.log(path, "->", err.name + ":", err.message);
  }
}

server.closeAllConnections();
server.close();
try {
  await fetch(`${base}/user`);
} catch (err) {
  console.log("server down ->", err.name + ":", err.message);
}
```

```text
$ node fetch.mjs
status 200 | ok: true | body: { id: 1, name: 'Ada' }
status 404 | ok: false | fetch did not reject
/user -> { id: 1, name: 'Ada' }
/nope -> Error: HTTP 404 for /nope
/slow -> TimeoutError: The operation was aborted due to timeout
server down -> TypeError: fetch failed
```

Four behaviours to take from this:

1. **`fetch` does not reject on 404 or 500.** It rejects only when the request cannot complete (network down, DNS failure, refused connection, abort). That is why the helper checks `res.ok` and throws its own error. This is the opposite of Python's `urllib` in the HTTP lesson, and a classic source of "my code treated an error page as data" bugs.
2. The body is read by a second promise: `await res.json()` (or `.text()`). It can fail too (invalid JSON), and a body can be read only once.
3. **`AbortSignal.timeout(300)`** cancelled the slow request and rejected with a `TimeoutError`. Without a timeout, a hung server can hang your code forever. `AbortController` gives manual cancellation (for example when a user navigates away).
4. When the server was down, `fetch` rejected with `TypeError: fetch failed` (the underlying cause is attached as `err.cause`). Network failures and HTTP errors are different failure kinds that deserve different handling (retry versus show a message).

### The three traps everyone falls into

```javascript
const sleep = (ms, value) => new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve(value), ms));

// Trap 1: forgetting await
const forgot = sleep(10, "the value");
console.log("without await:", forgot);
console.log("with await:   ", await forgot);

// Trap 2: async callbacks inside forEach are not awaited
const ids = [1, 2, 3];
ids.forEach(async (id) => {
  await sleep(10);
  console.log("forEach finished", id);
});
console.log("forEach returned (too early)");
await sleep(50);

// Fix: Promise.all over map (parallel) or for...of (one at a time)
await Promise.all(ids.map(async (id) => {
  await sleep(10);
  return id;
})).then((r) => console.log("Promise.all(map):", r));
for (const id of ids) {
  await sleep(5);
}
console.log("for...of finished in order");
```

```text
$ node traps.mjs
without await: Promise { <pending> }
with await:    the value
forEach returned (too early)
forEach finished 1
forEach finished 2
forEach finished 3
Promise.all(map): [ 1, 2, 3 ]
for...of finished in order
```

- **Forgot `await`:** you got `Promise { <pending> }` instead of the value. Using that object as if it were the value (`user.name` on a promise) gives `undefined`.
- **`forEach` with an `async` callback:** `forEach` ignores the returned promises, so it returned immediately and the work finished later, outside your control. Use `for...of` with `await` (one at a time, in order) or `Promise.all(items.map(async ...))` (all at once).
- **Awaiting inside a loop** serialises work that could run together. Use `Promise.all` when order does not matter, but bound the concurrency when the list is large (hundreds of simultaneous requests will hit rate limits).

And the one that crashes servers, a promise that rejects with nobody listening:

```text
$ node unhandled.mjs 2>&1 | head -n 4
file://~/demo/js/unhandled.mjs:2
  throw new Error("database is down");
        ^

$ node unhandled.mjs 2>/dev/null; echo "exit status: $?"
exit status: 1
```

`load()` is an `async` function that throws, and nobody awaited it or called `.catch`. Node.js prints the error and **terminates the process with exit status 1** (since Node 15 this is the default), so the "never printed" line never ran. Browsers instead log an `unhandledrejection` event and carry on. In both cases the fix is the same: every promise must be awaited, returned, or given a `.catch`.

## In real products

- **Browsers:** every `fetch`, image load and user interaction is asynchronous. UI frameworks (React, Vue, Svelte) and data libraries (TanStack Query, SWR) wrap these promises to give you loading and error states.
- **Node.js servers:** an Express or Fastify handler is typically `async (req, res) => { const user = await db.getUser(id); ... }`. One slow `await` only delays that request; but **CPU-heavy synchronous code blocks every request** (see the event-loop lesson).
- **Serverless and edge functions** use the same model, with strict time limits that make timeouts and cancellation mandatory.
- **Tooling:** build scripts, test runners and CLI tools use `fs/promises`, `child_process` and `fetch` with `await`.
- **Python** has the same ideas under `asyncio` (`async def`, `await`, `asyncio.gather`), so the mental model carries over.

> [!pitfall] Forgetting `await` or `return` inside an `async` function, or inside `.then`. The code "works" in tests that do not check the result, then fails later or silently. Turn on the lint rules `no-floating-promises` (typescript-eslint) and `require-await`, and make every promise either awaited, returned or `void`-ed on purpose.

> [!pitfall] `array.forEach(async ...)` and `array.map(async ...)` without `Promise.all`. The first never waits; the second gives you an array of promises, not values.

> [!pitfall] Using `try { return somePromise } catch {}` (without `await`) and expecting the catch to run. Rejections of a returned promise bypass the `catch`; write `return await somePromise` inside a `try` when you need to handle the error there.

> [!pitfall] Assuming `fetch` throws on HTTP errors, or reading the body twice. Check `res.ok`, read the body once, and keep the status code for your error message.

> [!tip] Prefer `async/await` for new code; keep `.then` for small transformations. Always handle errors at a boundary (a route handler, an event handler, a `main()`), add timeouts to every network call, and log the error with context (which request, which id).

> [!tip] Use `Promise.allSettled` when partial success is acceptable (loading several widgets), `Promise.all` when everything is needed, and a small concurrency limiter (the `p-limit` package, or processing in batches) for long lists of requests.

> [!cost] Concurrency is not free: many simultaneous requests use memory and sockets and can trigger rate limits or overload the other side, and unbounded `Promise.all` over thousands of items is a classic way to knock over a database. Retries multiply load, so use exponential backoff with jitter and a cap.

> [!security] Never trust data that arrives from a promise-returning call just because it parsed: validate it. Set timeouts and response-size limits for requests built from user input (otherwise an attacker can make your server fetch huge or never-ending responses), and do not forward user-controlled URLs to `fetch` without checking the destination (server-side request forgery). Do not log request headers that contain tokens.

> [!prod] Production checklist: a global handler for unhandled rejections that logs and exits cleanly (let the process manager restart it) instead of continuing in an unknown state; timeouts and cancellation on all outbound calls; bounded concurrency; retries only for idempotent requests; graceful shutdown that stops accepting work and awaits in-flight promises; and error reporting (such as Sentry) wired to both caught and uncaught async errors.

> [!version] Verified 2026-10-22 in the sandbox on Node.js v22.22.2: every script above was run as an ES module and the transcripts are the real output. The timings (600 ms and 200 ms) were rounded to the nearest 100 ms in the script because they vary by a few milliseconds; the unhandled-rejection transcript is shortened to its first four lines and the exit status, and the path was replaced by `~/demo`. NOT verified: browser behaviour (the `unhandledrejection` event, microtask ordering details in browsers), older Node versions (the default crash on unhandled rejections since Node 15, the `err.cause` detail on a failed `fetch`, `fetch` availability since Node 18 and `AbortSignal.timeout` since Node 17.3 and 16.14 are from the documentation as I know it, not from runs), the typescript-eslint and `p-limit` recommendations, and the linked resources, which could not be opened offline.

ts-core.md:
## Why it exists

JavaScript finds out that `user.adress.city` is a typo when a customer hits it in production. TypeScript adds a **static type checker** that reads your code before it runs and reports the typo at the keyboard. It is the standard way to build large JavaScript codebases: Next.js, Node services, React Native, and most SDKs ship TypeScript types.

**Mental model:** TypeScript is a very good linter with a model of your data's *shape*. It never changes how the program runs. The compiler checks, then **erases** every type annotation and emits plain JavaScript. That one fact explains most TypeScript surprises: types vanish at runtime, so anything arriving from outside (a network response, `JSON.parse`, an env var) is *unchecked* until you validate it.

{{dg:0}}

## How it works

**Structural typing.** Types are compatible when their *shapes* match, not when their names match. If a function wants `{ id: string }`, any object with a string `id` is accepted, whatever class or interface it came from. This is why TypeScript feels light, and also why two different ID strings are interchangeable unless you brand them.

**Inference first, annotations at boundaries.** Let inference type your local variables. Write explicit types on function parameters, exported function returns and public APIs: those are the contracts other code relies on.

**Unions and narrowing.** A value of type `string | number` can only use what both share until you *narrow* it with `typeof`, `in`, `instanceof`, equality or a custom type guard. The checker follows your `if` statements and knows the type inside each branch.

**Discriminated unions.** Give each variant a literal tag (`kind: "ok"`, `kind: "error"`). A `switch` on the tag narrows each case, and a `never` check in `default` makes the compiler fail when someone adds a new variant and forgets to handle it. This is the single most useful pattern for modelling states.

**`unknown` vs `any`.** `any` turns the checker off for that value and infects everything it touches. `unknown` means "could be anything, prove it before use". Use `unknown` for untrusted input and narrow it (usually with a schema library).

**Generics.** Type parameters let one function or type work across many types while keeping the relationship: `function first<T>(xs: readonly T[]): T | undefined`. Add constraints with `extends` (`<T extends { id: string }>`) when the body needs some shape.

**Utility, mapped and conditional types.** `Partial<T>`, `Required<T>`, `Pick<T, K>`, `Omit<T, K>`, `Record<K, V>`, `ReturnType<F>`, `Awaited<T>` derive new types from existing ones so you do not maintain two copies. Mapped types (`{ [K in keyof T]: ... }`) and conditional types (`T extends U ? X : Y`) are the machinery behind them; read them before you write them.

**`as const` and `satisfies`.** `as const` freezes a literal to its narrowest type (`"/orders/:id"` instead of `string`). `satisfies` checks that a value matches a type *without* widening it, so you get validation and precise inference at once.

**Branded types.** Intersect a primitive with a phantom tag so `UserId` and `OrderId` are not interchangeable even though both are strings. Zero runtime cost; the brand is erased.

**`tsconfig` strictness.** `"strict": true` is the baseline. Add `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` (array and record lookups may be `undefined`), `exactOptionalPropertyTypes`, `noImplicitOverride` and `verbatimModuleSyntax` (explicit `import type`, predictable emit). TypeScript 6.0 changed several defaults and 7.0 turns some previously deprecated options into hard errors, so read the release notes before upgrading an old config.

**The compiler is now native.** TypeScript 7.0 (released around July 2026) is a port of the compiler and language service to Go. Microsoft reports roughly 8x to 12x faster full builds on large projects (VS Code's own codebase: about 125.7 s on 6.0 down to 10.6 s on 7.0) while keeping the same type system. TypeScript 6.0 was the bridge release and the last one on the JavaScript codebase. Tools that embed TypeScript through its programmatic API (Vue, Svelte, Astro, MDX, Angular template checking) stay on 6.x until a stable API arrives in a later 7.x release. Nightly builds still ship as `@typescript/native-preview` with the `tsgo` binary.

**Running `.ts` directly.** Recent Node versions can strip types and run `.ts` files without a build step, for *erasable* syntax only (no `enum`, no `namespace`, no parameter properties). TypeScript's `erasableSyntaxOnly` option enforces that. Check your Node version's docs for the exact flag and default (from memory).

## Worked example

Model the data so impossible states cannot exist, and validate at the edge with a runtime schema. The schema is the source of truth; the static type is derived from it, so they cannot drift.

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

// 1. Runtime schema at the boundary; static type derived from it.
const Order = z.object({
  id: z.string().min(1),
  status: z.enum(["pending", "paid", "shipped", "refunded"]),
  totalCents: z.number().int().nonnegative(),
});
type Order = z.infer<typeof Order>;

// 2. A discriminated union makes illegal states unrepresentable.
type Load<T> =
  | { kind: "idle" }
  | { kind: "loading" }
  | { kind: "ok"; data: T }
  | { kind: "error"; message: string };

// 3. Exhaustive switch: adding a new `kind` above breaks the build here.
function describe(s: Load<Order>): string {
  switch (s.kind) {
    case "idle":    return "Nothing yet";
    case "loading": return "Loading…";
    case "ok":      return `Order ${s.data.id} is ${s.data.status}`;
    case "error":   return `Failed: ${s.message}`;
    default: {
      const unreachable: never = s;
      return unreachable;
    }
  }
}

// 4. `unknown` in, validated value out. No `as Order` lie.
async function getOrder(id: string): Promise<Load<Order>> {
  try {
    const res = await fetch(`/api/orders/${encodeURIComponent(id)}`);
    if (!res.ok) return { kind: "error", message: `HTTP ${res.status}` };
    const json: unknown = await res.json();
    const parsed = Order.safeParse(json);
    return parsed.success
      ? { kind: "ok", data: parsed.data }
      : { kind: "error", message: "Unexpected response shape" };
  } catch (e) {
    return { kind: "error", message: e instanceof Error ? e.message : "Network error" };
  }
}
```

Branded IDs, so you cannot pass a user id where an order id is expected:

```ts
type Brand<T, B extends string> = T & { readonly __brand: B };
type UserId = Brand<string, "UserId">;
type OrderId = Brand<string, "OrderId">;

const asUserId = (s: string) => s as UserId;     // the ONE place a cast is allowed
const asOrderId = (s: string) => s as OrderId;

function cancelOrder(order: OrderId, by: UserId) { /* ... */ }

cancelOrder(asOrderId("o_1"), asUserId("u_9"));  // ok
// cancelOrder(asUserId("u_9"), asOrderId("o_1")); // compile error: arguments swapped
```

`satisfies` and `as const` for a route table that stays precise:

```ts
const routes = {
  home: "/",
  order: "/orders/:id",
  settings: "/settings",
} as const satisfies Record<string, `/${string}`>;

type RouteName = keyof typeof routes;      // "home" | "order" | "settings"
type OrderPath = typeof routes["order"];   // "/orders/:id" (not just string)
```

A strict baseline `tsconfig.json` for an app whose bundler does the emitting:

```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "strict": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "noEmit": true
  },
  "include": ["src"]
}
```

## In real products

- **Next.js and Vite** apps use TypeScript by default. Bundlers (SWC, esbuild, Turbopack) only strip types and do not check them, so CI must run `tsc --noEmit` (or `tsgo --noEmit`) as its own step.
- **Types from schemas:** `supabase gen types typescript` from your database, Prisma and Drizzle from their schemas, `openapi-typescript` from an OpenAPI file, GraphQL Code Generator from a GraphQL schema. Generate rather than hand-write anything that mirrors a server contract.
- **Zod (or Valibot, ArkType)** at every trust boundary: request bodies, env vars, webhook payloads, LLM structured output. `z.infer` gives you the type for free.
- **tRPC and typed clients** share types between server and client so a renamed field breaks the build, not production.
- **Libraries** ship `.d.ts` declaration files; for untyped packages, DefinitelyTyped provides `@types/*`.

| Choice | Use when | Cost |
|---|---|---|
| `unknown` + schema validation | any data from outside your process | a few lines per boundary |
| `as T` assertion | you truly know more than the checker (rare, localized) | silent lie if you are wrong |
| `any` | almost never; migrating legacy code | disables checking and spreads |
| union of string literals | fixed sets of values | none at runtime |
| `enum` | interop with existing enum-based code | emits runtime code, breaks erasable-only mode |
| `interface` vs `type` | `interface` for extendable object contracts; `type` for unions and computed types | little difference day to day; be consistent |
| Project references and `composite` | monorepos, faster incremental builds | more config |

> [!pitfall] `as` assertions and `JSON.parse(...) as Foo` do not validate anything. The type is erased at runtime, so a wrong cast ships a crash. Parse with a schema instead.

> [!pitfall] `enum` emits real JavaScript and has surprising numeric behavior. Prefer a union of string literals (`type Status = "paid" | "refunded"`) or an `as const` object.

> [!pitfall] Turning on `strict` late in a project produces thousands of errors. Migrate by directory, or enable flags one at a time, and use `// @ts-expect-error` (which fails when the error disappears) rather than `// @ts-ignore`.

> [!security] Type safety is not input validation. A perfectly typed handler still trusts attacker-controlled JSON until you validate it; the same goes for prototype-pollution-style keys in merged objects. Validate and strip unknown keys at the edge.

> [!prod] Run the type check, lint and tests as separate CI jobs so failures are attributable, and cache `.tsbuildinfo` for incremental builds. With the native compiler the type-check job often shrinks from minutes to seconds.

> [!tip] Hover and "go to type definition" are your debugger for types. When a type is unreadable, assign it to a named alias in a scratch file and inspect it piece by piece.

> [!cost] Deeply recursive conditional or mapped types slow the checker and the editor. If completions lag, simplify the type before blaming the machine.
